1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:09,192
Debugger stuck in SmsClient.<init>. The
cinema (and every other product in the
building) uses a third-party SMS SDK.

2
00:00:09,311 --> 00:00:23,039
You cannot put @Component on
com.vendor.sms.SmsClient — you do not own
the source, and the vendor constructor
needs an API key plus a timeout the
container will not invent. Stereotype
scanning is the wrong door.

3
00:00:23,159 --> 00:00:35,831
@Bean is the door that fits: a factory
method you write, returning an object
Spring then manages. A @Bean method is a
factory method whose return value Spring
registers as a container-managed object.

4
00:00:35,951 --> 00:00:48,527
Method name defaults to the bean name.
Parameters are injected from the
container. You can declare initMethod /
destroyMethod for objects that expose
lifecycle without Spring annotations.

5
00:00:48,647 --> 00:01:02,543
This is how you adapt the world you do not
control into the graph you do — SDKs,
legacy singletons, ObjectMapper
customizations, connection clients. Look
at this java for a moment. Walk the
factory.

6
00:01:02,663 --> 00:01:10,079
smsClient(Environment env) receives the
Environment bean as a parameter — no field
injection required.

7
00:01:10,199 --> 00:01:18,935
getRequiredProperty("sms.api-key") fails
fast if the key is missing;
getProperty(..., 5) supplies a default
timeout when unset.

8
00:01:19,055 --> 00:01:25,847
new SmsClient() is ordinary construction
inside *your* method; returning client
hands the instance to Spring.

9
00:01:25,967 --> 00:01:38,831
initMethod = "start" means after the
method returns and the object is
registered, Spring reflects and calls
start() on it. destroyMethod = "shutdown"
registers a destruction callback for
context close.

10
00:01:38,951 --> 00:01:46,607
bookingNotifier(SmsClient smsClient) asks
the container for the smsClient singleton
by type — one client,

11
00:01:46,727 --> 00:01:54,311
shared — and wraps it in an app-owned
SmsBookingNotifier that your domain can
depend on without importing vendor
packages everywhere.

12
00:01:54,431 --> 00:02:06,551
Runtime during refresh. Spring defines
beans smsClient and bookingNotifier from
method metadata. When creating smsClient,
it invokes your method, then start.

13
00:02:06,671 --> 00:02:15,575
If start opens a vendor connection pool
and throws, context refresh fails — you
see the exception during startup, not on
the first booking confirmation.

14
00:02:15,695 --> 00:02:29,423
Dependents like bookingNotifier are
created afterward (or as needed) with the
same instance injected. On
ConfigurableApplicationContext.close(),
Spring calls shutdown even though the
vendor never heard of @PreDestroy.

15
00:02:29,543 --> 00:02:37,199
Your notifier can stay a plain class; only
the configuration module imports
com.vendor.sms.SmsClient.

16
00:02:37,319 --> 00:02:46,271
@Bean also covers conditional registration
(@ConditionalOnProperty), @Profile on
methods, and @Primary / @Qualifier on the
return.

17
00:02:46,391 --> 00:02:57,792
Example: local profile returns a
ConsoleBookingNotifier from another @Bean
method while prod returns
SmsBookingNotifier — same BookingNotifier
type, different instances gated by
profile.

18
00:02:57,912 --> 00:03:10,656
It is not a lesser cousin of @Component.
It is the escape hatch and the integration
seam. Failure mode with clear symptoms:
omit destroyMethod (and the vendor has no
Spring lifecycle hooks).

19
00:03:10,776 --> 00:03:22,920
On every Boot DevTools restart or test
context close, SMS connections leak until
the vendor's remote side rate-limits you —
symptom is "too many open sessions" from
the SMS API after a day of local restarts,

20
00:03:23,040 --> 00:03:27,192
while production looks fine because pods
recycle less often.

21
00:03:27,312 --> 00:03:38,640
Another failure: call new SmsClient()
inside SmsBookingNotifier's constructor
instead of injecting the @Bean — two
clients, two API sessions, initMethod only
ran on the unused bean.

22
00:03:38,760 --> 00:03:53,472
Symptom: metrics show double outbound SMS
traffic or duplicate confirmations. Trade-
offs. @Bean localizes foreign construction
and lifecycle in one config class — easy
to audit, easy to fake in
@TestConfiguration.

23
00:03:53,592 --> 00:04:06,288
You must remember destroy callbacks for
resources the vendor expects closed.
@Component on *your* types is shorter when
you own the source and need only
constructor injection; it cannot wrap
sealed third-party classes.

24
00:04:06,408 --> 00:04:15,672
Returning interface types
(BookingNotifier) from @Bean methods hides
vendor types from the rest of the app —
worth the extra wrapper when the SDK is
noisy.

25
00:04:15,792 --> 00:04:25,512
Misconception unique to @Bean: "Returning
new from @Bean means Spring does not
manage the object — it is unmanaged
because I constructed it." False.

26
00:04:25,632 --> 00:04:34,367
You constructed it inside the factory
method; Spring still applies scope,
lifecycle callbacks you declared, and
injects that instance into dependents.

27
00:04:34,487 --> 00:04:43,031
Management starts when the method returns
into the container, not when the bytecode
used new. SMS works in one environment.

28
00:04:43,151 --> 00:04:56,303
Staging and production need different API
keys, and local dev wants a console
notifier instead of real texts. Same
artifact, different bean sets — that is
the profile problem waiting behind the
configuration class.
